      "prompt": "Canvas and aspect ratio: Create a single 9:21 vertical image.\n\nOptional reference image usage: Use Image 1 as the product identity reference. Infer the exact product appearance from Image 1 and keep the same product identity; do not redesign, invent, or over-describe the product from text. If a 1:3 master reference image is also provided, use it only as a low-strength visual style anchor for palette, warm lighting, baked material atmosphere, and product mood. Do not copy its layout, do not crop it, and do not compress the current 9:21 page structure.\n\nOverall visual system: Build a warm healing bakery world with a creamy beige background, baked golden tones, rich chocolate brown accents, and soft milk white highlights. Use gentle backlight and side volumetric light like afternoon tea sunlight. Create a playful 3D miniature floating space with soft depth of field, realistic appetizing cookie texture, glossy chocolate pieces, small cookie crumbs, cocoa elements, and a few silky milk droplets. Keep the commercial mood warm, sweet, clean, and appetite-driven.\n\nCurrent viewport: Slice 01, the opening hero viewport. The commercial task is to make customers instantly understand the product identity, remember the visible chocolate richness, and want to continue scrolling.\n\nScreen job and evidence: Use a strong product-led visual plus up to three light benefit tags. The visual evidence should come from the product from Image 1, with the chocolate pieces, golden baked surface, crumbs, and warm bakery atmosphere clearly supporting the appetite claim.\n\nLayout intent: Create an elegant hero opening with generous negative space in the upper area and a floating product-led center of attention. Avoid dense card stacking. The headline should sit in the upper breathing space, either centered or slightly left-aligned, without covering the product.\n\nFlexible page composition: Use one dominant main visual occupying about the central visual weight, one small benefit tag group around the product at lower contrast, and one small product identity anchor near the lower safe area. Maintain one strongest headline and one strongest visual focus; all secondary elements must be smaller, softer, and visually subordinate. The page should feel premium and airy, not like a PPT information layout.\n\nPrimary module: Place the product from Image 1 as the largest focal object in a subtle floating bakery space. Let surrounding chocolate pieces, fine crumbs, and a few milk droplets form a light circular or diagonal flow around it. Keep the product crisp, realistic, appetizing, and clearly recognizable from Image 1. Do not create a separate hard product poster or opaque bottom strip.\n\nSecondary modules: Add up to three small rounded integrated tags floating near the product in low-weight areas, using a pale cream base and chocolate-brown short text. Add a small product identity anchor near the lower safe area, showing the same product identity from Image 1 at smaller scale, clearly secondary to the main cookie visual. The tag group and identity anchor should feel embedded in the warm scene rather than placed inside stacked white cards.\n\nChinese typography: Use visible Chinese text only. visible Chinese headline: \"看得见的大颗巧克力\" ; visible Chinese subheadline: \"一口酥脆，甜蜜治愈刚刚好\" ; visible Chinese tags: \"酥脆饼体\", \"浓郁巧克力豆\", \"下午茶小满足\". Typography lock: use one consistent modern Chinese sans-serif style across the screen, preferably close to Source Han Sans, Alibaba PuHuiTi, or HarmonyOS Sans. Headlines must be rounded, full, friendly, medium-bold or semi-bold, with clean stable strokes and no changing weight style. Body text should use regular weight, high readability, and minimal small text. Use no more than three hierarchy levels: headline, subheadline, tags or short notes. Keep alignment unified within the screen, preferably centered or left-aligned but not mixed. Use natural tracking, comfortable line spacing, no negative tracking, no stretched letters, no script font, no serif Songti-style font, no handwriting, no English visible title, no garbled text, no excessive outline, no heavy shadow, and no mixed font families.\n\nEdge safety: Keep the top 10%-15% as a warm creamy gradient and soft glow transition area, with enough breathing room around the headline. Keep the bottom 10%-15% as golden crumbs, creamy wave-like gradient, and a few chocolate pieces guiding the viewer downward. Do not place important text or the main product too close to the edges.\n\nComposition notes: Use a polished ecommerce hero composition with shallow depth of field, soft highlights on chocolate pieces, realistic crumb texture, and a gentle floating rhythm. The eye flow should move from headline to the main product, then to the small benefit tags and lower product identity anchor. The image should feel appetizing, warm, and emotionally soothing.\n\nImportant constraints: Image 1 identity has priority over all text instructions. Do not redesign the product, do not turn it into another snack type, do not invent unrelated packaging or shapes, and do not over-describe product structure. All visible text must be Simplified Chinese only. Do not render English titles, English small print, workflow words, page labels, module labels, navigation words, search words, field names, or placeholder text. No cold palette, no flat vector look, no pistachio green, no medical or meal-replacement wording, no hard horizontal bar, no full-screen opaque color block, no white PPT-like card stack, no three equal icon cards, no exaggerated promo button, and no garbled text.",
