      "prompt": "Canvas and aspect ratio: Create a single 9:21 vertical image.\n\nOptional reference image usage: Use Image 1 as the product identity reference. Infer the product appearance from Image 1 and keep the same product identity; do not redesign, invent, or over-describe the product from text. If a 1:3 master reference image is also provided, use it only as a low-strength visual style anchor for palette, lighting, material atmosphere, and product mood. Do not copy its layout, do not crop it, and do not reduce the current 9:21 page structure.\n\nOverall visual system: Continue the warm healing bakery world from the opening screen: creamy beige background, baked golden tones, deep chocolate brown details, milk white softness, gentle afternoon side backlight, and subtle volumetric glow. Use a 3D commercial illustration feeling blended with appetizing realistic cookie details. Keep floating chocolate pieces, golden crumbs, and soft milk-toned highlights as continuity motifs, but place them naturally within a lifestyle scene.\n\nCurrent viewport: Slice 02, a lifestyle continuation viewport. The commercial task is to move the hero appetite appeal into a believable afternoon tea, office break, or relaxed sharing moment, showing why the product feels pleasant to keep nearby.\n\nScreen job and evidence: Show a positive human usage result and emotional benefit. The evidence should come from a relaxed consumer moment, clear product-use relationship, warm tabletop atmosphere, and a small pairing detail with coffee or milk. Keep the scene light and desirable, not problem-oriented.\n\nLayout intent: Use a full-bleed lifestyle scene rather than a product card page. The person and hand action form the primary visual focus, while the product from Image 1 appears naturally in use. Avoid bottom white cards, hard split screens, equal scene grids, old-solution comparisons, or any grey negative pain-point setup.\n\nFlexible page composition: Use one main lifestyle scene occupying most of the viewport, one small emotional note integrated near the hand and product area, and one smaller shallow-depth detail moment within the same environment. Maintain a single strongest headline and one main scene focus. Secondary text and detail should be small, soft, and blended into the warm light, not equal-weight cards.\n\nPrimary module: Create a warm afternoon tea or office break tabletop scene. A generic young consumer, preferably shown with partial face, hands, or relaxed body crop, naturally picks up the product from Image 1. The product should be clear and appetizing but not isolated inside a product description card. Include a cup of coffee or milk nearby, soft table texture, a few golden crumbs, and chocolate pieces in the environment. The expression and body language should feel relaxed, satisfied, and quietly happy.\n\nSecondary modules: Add a light short note close to the hand-and-cookie area, following the tabletop perspective and warm light. Add a small natural close-up detail within the same scene, such as a half piece of the same product near a coffee or milk cup, with visible crumbs and chocolate pieces softly highlighted. These secondary modules must feel like depth-of-field scene details or gentle floating notes, not separate white cards.\n\nChinese typography: Use visible Chinese text only. visible Chinese headline: \"金黄酥脆，唤醒午后食欲\" ; visible Chinese subheadline: \"浓郁巧克力豆藏进饼体，每一口都像刚好被治愈\" ; visible Chinese tags: \"办公室休息\", \"下午茶搭配\", \"朋友分享\". Typography lock: use one consistent modern Chinese sans-serif style across the screen, preferably close to Source Han Sans, Alibaba PuHuiTi, or HarmonyOS Sans. Headlines must be rounded, full, friendly, medium-bold or semi-bold, with clean stable strokes and no changing weight style. Body text should use regular weight, high readability, and minimal small text. Use no more than three hierarchy levels: headline, subheadline, tags or short notes. Keep alignment unified within the screen, preferably left-aligned or centered but not mixed. Use natural tracking, comfortable line spacing, no negative tracking, no stretched letters, no script font, no serif Songti-style font, no handwriting, no English visible title, no garbled text, no excessive outline, no heavy shadow, and no mixed font families.\n\nEdge safety: The top 10%-15% should gently inherit the creamy wave and floating crumb feeling from the previous slice before opening into the tabletop warm-light scene. The headline should not touch the top edge. The bottom 10%-15% should keep warm tabletop color, a soft cup edge, crumbs, and a few chocolate pieces to guide the next mechanism or texture-focused slice. Keep hands, faces, product, and text safely away from crop edges.\n\nComposition notes: Use a different visual grammar from Slice 01. Instead of a centered floating hero product, use an immersive full-bleed lifestyle moment with diagonal tabletop flow, warm side light, realistic shallow depth of field, and soft 3D commercial polish. The eye should move from headline to hand-and-product action, then to the pairing detail and small tags. Keep the screen medium-density with comfortable breathing space.\n\nImportant constraints: Image 1 identity has priority. Do not redesign the product, do not place it inside a white product explanation card, and do not replace it with another snack. If a person appears, use a generic model or partial human crop; do not copy a reference person's face, identity, clothing details, brand, original product, or exact pose. All visible text must be Simplified Chinese only. Do not render English titles, English small print, workflow words, page labels, module labels, navigation words, search words, field names, or placeholders. No black-and-white pain comparison, no old-solution comparison, no grey negative scene, no search page, no summary page, no left-right split, no bottom description card, no three-icon card row, no cold palette, no flat vector style, no pistachio green, no medical or meal-replacement wording, no hard horizontal bar, and no garbled text.",
