ule occupying about the middle 55-60% of the viewport, with three smaller supporting evidence zones arranged asymmetrically along a soft diagonal aroma flow. The primary module must be the most visually powerful; supporting modules should be smaller, lighter, semi-transparent, and lower contrast. Fill the tall screen with layered depth, macro windows, aroma lines, floating ingredients, and negative space at the top and bottom.\n\nPrimary module: Show the product from Image 1 as a large partial close-up with a refined local enlargement and gentle cutaway impression. Golden bakery light descends from the upper area and visually seeps into the product surface. Warm aroma lines emerge near edges and natural cracks. Keep the product identity consistent with Image 1 and let image2 infer any reasonable close-up angle from Image 1.\n\nSecondary modules: Add one small macro evidence inset near the side showing surface texture, baked cracks, crumbs, and golden color from the same product identity. Add one ingredient evidence path where chocolate chips and cocoa crumbs float diagonally toward the main product. Add one small result cue near the lower area with cream-colored aroma waves and a few milk droplets, suggesting an afternoon-tea pairing. These supporting elements must not become equal-weight cards or a dense icon wall.\n\nChinese typography: All visible text must be Simplified Chinese only. visible Chinese headline: \"像刚出炉一样香\" ; visible Chinese subheadline: \"匠心烘焙出金黄酥感，掰开就有温暖香气。\" ; visible Chinese tags: \"金黄烘焙\", \"酥香裂纹\", \"香气联想\". Use a consistent modern Chinese sans-serif style close to Source Han Sans, Alibaba PuHuiTi, or HarmonyOS Sans. The headline should be rounded, full, friendly, medium-bold or semi-bold with stable strokes. Body text should use regular weight and remain highly readable. Use at most three text levels: headline, subheadline, and short tags. Keep one alignment system within the screen, preferably left aligned or centered but not mixed. Natural