isual center.\n\nFlexible page composition: Use one strong primary visual occupying about 60% of the screen, with two smaller taste-layer cues and one tiny lifestyle cue integrated into the scene. Do not use three equal cards, white function cards, surrounding icon rows, bottom explanation strips, or a repeated centered hero layout. The supporting cues should feel like light floating annotations in the environment, visibly secondary to the headline and diagonal product scene.\n\nPrimary module: Create a diagonal full-scene composition with the same product from Image 1 appearing naturally in depth, from a crisp foreground piece to softer background pieces. Use warm highlights, shallow depth of field, floating crumbs, and converging cocoa-and-wheat aroma trails to show layered flavor. Do not reuse the same close-up scale or product angle from the previous slice.\n\nSecondary modules: Add a small cocoa-layer cue near the chocolate-brown trail with glossy chocolate chips and cocoa fragments. Add a small texture-layer cue near the golden crumb trail with delicate crumbs separating from the cookie edge to suggest crispness. Add a subtle lower-scene benefit cue with a blurred coffee cup and milk cup, plus a partial hand or tabletop interaction only as a light lifestyle signal; the product remains the hero.\n\nChinese typography: All visible text must be Simplified Chinese only. visible Chinese headline: \"可可浓香，麦香酥脆\" ; visible Chinese subheadline: \"浓郁巧克力豆碰撞烘焙麦香，甜而不单调。\" ; visible Chinese tags: \"双重口感\", \"层层递进\", \"下午茶好搭档\". Use a consistent modern Chinese sans-serif style close to Source Han Sans, Alibaba PuHuiTi, or HarmonyOS Sans. The headline should be rounded, full, friendly, medium-bold or semi-bold with stable strokes. Body text should use regular weight and remain clear. Use at most three text levels: headline, subheadline, and short tags. Keep text alignment consistent within this screen, with natural tracking and relaxed line spacing. Do not use Songti-style serif, handwriting, calligraphy, English serif, heavy outline, strong shadow, negative tracking, stretched type, or mixed font families.\n\nEdge safety: Keep the top 12-15% airy with cream light, a few golden crumbs, and soft milk-white wave continuation from the previous slice. Keep headline and important product details away from the edges. Use the bottom 12-15% for softly blurred coffee, milk, and warm tabletop edges, leaving a gentle transition toward the next detail-focused screen.\n\nComposition notes: The eye flow should travel diagonally through the product scene, then split into the cocoa trail and wheat-crisp trail, finally landing on the beverage-sharing cue. Maintain a clean commercial hierarchy: one dominant headline, one main diagonal food scene, two small flavor tags, and one light scene-benefit tag. Use realistic food texture, warm bokeh, soft shadows, and appetizing golden highlights.\n\nImportant constraints: Image 1 identity has priority. Do not redesign the product, do not turn it into another food category, and do not invent packaging or brand details. Do not make a technical cutaway again, do not use the same centered macro product angle as slice 03, and do not create a single-product poster. Do not use cold colors, medical or diet wording, green pistachio tones, white PPT-like cards, hard horizontal bars, dense tiny text, parameter tables, certification graphics, complex icon groups, black-and-white comparisons, English visible text, or garbled text. Never render internal blueprint labels, workflow words, page type names, UI/search/navigation words, or structural placeholders as visible text.",
